fill the panel with a nes or snes picture

A NES or SNES game now opens full screen: the picture is drawn across the
whole panel, 752x560 on the Flip and 640x480 on the Mini. Both panels are
4:3 or within 1% of it, which is the shape a television showed these
consoles at -- their pixels were 8:7, not square, and the TV overscanned
the edges -- so filling the panel is the console's own aspect rather than
a distortion. It is 2.94x across by 2.5x down on the Flip, nearest-neighbour,
so columns and rows come out two and three pixels wide in turn.

This changes what the player sees, on purpose, and is a feature rather
than something upstream needs to work here; upstream has no NES or SNES.

Stretch is what draws it, and is now the default for the two consoles: a
cart with no line in Config/video_mode.txt opens stretched, where a Game
Boy family cart still opens at actual size. `video_mode_for` takes the
platform to know which. Actual stays the 2x pixel-exact picture with a
border; R1 picks it on a NES game and L1 goes back. The SNES leaves its
shoulders to the game, so there `actual` in video_mode.txt is the way back.

`the_effective_scale_is_never_doubled_up` asked every mode for square
pixels; a stretched console is let off that, and still held to the panel.

Not yet run on the handheld. Filling the panel is more output pixels than
2x (421,120 against 229,376) and more than the 640x560 stretch it replaces,
so the game pass costs more per frame; time it with the rest of
notes/nes-snes.md.

Applies to upstream 91499a9, after 0001-0027.

